alue versus retaining all existing plans. For Zurich only at low growth rate: existing £102,000 RIY 0.3%; new solution £100,000 RIY 0.6%; critical yield 0.3%; effect of switching £2,000 or 2.0% decrease. For the full comparison the new solution shows 0.6% RIY versus retained Zurich at 0.29% and retained Aviva at 0.59%. The file notes confirm: Scottish Widows discounted for no servicing and reduced flexibility; Aviva internal discounted as only mixed asset fund is risk rated 4; Zurich internal discounted as no risk level 5 funds and blend underperformed; Standard Life internal discounted as all funds more expensive than Aviva. The Zurich switch specifically shows a higher RIY (0.6% vs 0.3%) and a projected decrease in maturity value at low growth rate of £2,000 (2.0%). The suitability report states for Zurich that cost is not the primary justification and instead relies on investment choice and flexibility benefits. The charge comparison table in the suitability report shows Aviva existing 0.50% vs new solution 0.44% (impact -0.06%), and separately a table showing Zurich existing charges. The Defaqto switching report confirms the new solution RIY of 0.6% across all scenarios, which is higher than the Zurich existing RIY of 0.3%.","discrepancies":["The suitability report states for the Aviva switch 'The plan itself is 0.06% more expensive than the recommended solution' — this wording is ambiguous and potentially contradictory: if 'the plan' refers to the existing Aviva plan, this is consistent with the charge table showing existing 0.50% vs new 0.44% (-0.06%). However, the phrase 'the plan itself is 0.06% more expensive than the recommended solution' could be read as the recommended solution being 0.06% more expensive, which would contradict the lower-cost claim.","The Zurich-only switching analysis in the Defaqto report shows that at low growth rate the new solution produces a lower projected value (£100,000 vs £102,000) and a higher RIY (0.6% vs 0.3%), with critical