"analysis":"The suitability report does disclose charges for the recommended solution (0.21% platform + 0.22% investment management = 0.44% total) and contains a pension switch charges table comparing existing and new charges for each plan being switched. Non-cost reasons are documented for discounting alternatives (Scottish Widows, Aviva internal, Zurich internal, Standard Life internal), and the file notes corroborate these reasons. A guard applies: cheaper options rejected for evidenced non-cost reasons (fund range, access, servicing) are a valid outcome, and the evidence supports the non-cost justifications for Zurich, Scottish Widows, and Aviva internal.\n\nHowever, there is a material concern: the Defaqto switching report shows that for the Zurich switch specifically, the new solution carries a higher RIY (0.6% vs 0.3%) and produces a lower projected value at low growth rate. The suitability report's charge comparison table appears to show a cost saving (existing Zurich 0.93% vs impact 0.26%), but this reflects only the difference in product/platform/investment charges — the RIY in the Defaqto report captures the cumulative effect including advice charges, and that figure is higher for the new solution. The suitability report does not explicitly reconcile this discrepancy or quantify what investment outperformance or non-cost benefits are required to justify the higher overall charge burden on the Zurich fund (which at £103,439 is by far the largest fund being switched).\n\nThe ambiguous wording 'The plan itself is 0.06% more expensive than the recommended solution' in the Aviva switch section could mislead a reader into thinking the new solution is more expensive, when the intent appears to be the opposite. This is a low-level concern given the context makes the direction of saving apparent from the charge table.\n\nThe most significant issue is the absence of an explicit acknowledgement in the suitability report that the Zurich switch results in a higher RIY