nced ongoing 0.29%, transaction 0.26%, total 0.55%; aggregate fund costs £406.74 (0.37%). The suitability report fund table (P22) shows only annual charges of 0.18% and 0.29% respectively and does not disclose transaction costs. (3) P7 (policy research) confirms several existing arrangements (Aviva My Future Focus, Scottish Widows) are invested in lifestyle strategies. P21 (suitability report) references Zurich's fund limitation — no risk level 5 funds, requiring a blend of risk 7 and risk 2, supporting the lifestyling/investment choice benefit for Zurich. (4) P19 (suitability report) confirms Scottish Widows and People's Pension do not allow a servicing adviser; Aviva Pension Portfolio does (via facilitated adviser charging per P4 Key Features). (5) P4 and P8 (Aviva Key Features) confirm flexi-access drawdown, UFPLS, annuity and phased drawdown options, supporting the retirement flexibility benefit. (6) P19 comparison table shows People's Pension offers FAD and UFPLS; Scottish Widows offers UFPLS only; existing Aviva offers FAD and UFPLS; Zurich offers UFPLS only; Standard Life offers FAD. So the flexibility benefit is genuine for Zurich and Scottish Widows but partially already available in People's Pension and existing Aviva. (7) The SIPP research report (P3, P12) identifies Aviva Pension Portfolio — Choice as the recommended product from a panel of 118, supporting product selection rationale. (8) No provider illustration for the recommended Aviva plan is present in the evidence pack to confirm projected values, RIY or critical yield calculations. (9) The critical yield is stated as 4.8% in the report narrative but the charge arithmetic implies 4.24% based on the stated £4,672 required outperformance against £110,188 fund value. (10) The CT Universal MAP transaction costs (0.26%, £145.05) are material but not disclosed in the suitability report's fund charge table.","discrepancies":["Report claims 'lower overall cost structure' as a benefit on page 7, but the report itself contains an internal inconsistency on page 6 stating 'The plan itself is 0.06% more expensive than the recommended solution' which contradicts the lower cost claim (though the switch charges table shows 0.44% new vs 0.50% existing, implying the new is cheaper — the text contradiction is unresolved).","Critical yield stated as 4.8% in the report narrative (page 11) but charge arithmetic implies 4.24% based on the figures extracted from the canonical model, creating an unreconciled discrepancy.","Fund charge table in the suitability report (page 12/P22) discloses only annual management charges (HSBC 0.18%, CT Universal MAP 0.29%) but does not disclose transaction costs; P10 shows CT Universal MAP transaction costs of 0.26% (£145.05) which are material and would increase the total fund cost to 0.55% for that fund — the aggregate disclosed in P10 is 0.37% total vs 0.24% ongoing only.","The solution charge arithmetic is applied to a fund value of ~£110,185 (transfer value to Aviva) not the aggregate of all existing arrangements (£116,998.47); the lower-cost benefit comparison is therefore not applied to the full existing arrangement base.","Enhanced Retirement Flexibility benefit is stated as a general benefit of the new scheme, but People's Pension (retained, not transferred) and existing Aviva already offer FAD and UFPLS, meaning the flexibility benefit is not uniformly additional across all ceding plans.","No provider illustration for the recommended Aviva Pension Portfolio is present in the evidence pack, so projected values, RIY and critical yield cannot be independently verified against illustration documentation.","The ongoing review service described on page 16 (ATR review, investment suitability, market updates, ad-hoc advice, holistic planning) is asserted as justification for the ongoing adviser charge but no client agreement or fee disclosure document confirming the ongoing adviser charge rate is present in the evidence pack to verify the service proposition matches the charge agreed."],"comparisonPerformed":true,"missingInputs":["Provider illustration for the recommended Aviva Pension Portfolio (needed to verify projected values, RIY, critical yield of 4.8%)","Client agreement or ongoing service agreement confirming the ongoing adviser charge rate and service proposition"],"analysis":"The report relies on seven stated benefits and four cost-justification assertions to support the recommendation. Several of these are corroborated by the evidence: the lifestyling risk benefit is supported by P7 confirming existing arrangements (including Aviva My Future Focus and Scottish Widows) are in lifestyle strategies; the investment choice benefit for Zurich is supported by P21's own acknowledgement that Zurich lacks risk-5 funds; the adviser charging facilitation benefit is supported by P4 Key Features and P19 comparison table showing ceding plans do not allow servicing advisers; retirement flexibility is supported by P4/P8 Key Features and P19 comparison table, though this benefit is already partially available on People's Pension and existing Aviva, limiting its incremental value as a justification. The multi-asset diversification and ongoing monitoring benefits are generic and not contradicted by evidence, though their value as specific justifications is limited. However, three material concerns arise. First, the lower cost claim — the primary financial justification — is undermined by an internal contradiction in the report itself (page 6 text vs the switch charges table), which is flagged in the canonical model's internalInconsistencies. The guard on false positives does not apply here because the contradiction is within the report's own pages, not between the report and an external document. Second, the CT Universal MAP transaction costs of 0.26% disclosed in P10 are not disclosed in the suitability report's fund charge table, meaning the aggregated all-in cost presented to the client is understated. This is a moderate omission because the MiFID II pre-sale disclosure in P10 does contain this figure and was presumably provided to the client, but the suitability report itself does not draw attention to it or explain its impact on the cost comparison. Third, the critical yield figure is inconsistent between the report narrative (4.8%) and the arithmetic implied by the canonical model (4.24%), and no provider illustration is available to resolve this. The absence of a provider illustration also means the projected value and RIY cannot be verified. The vulnerability overlay: no vulnerability characteristics are identified in the evidence that would materially alter this assessment. Overall, the lower-cost benefit claim — the most prominent justification — is self-contradictory within the report, transaction costs for one fund are not disclosed in the suitability report charge table, and the critical yield figure cannot be reconciled or verified against an illustration. These are material concerns.","citations":[{"passageId":"P21","source":"Suitability Report Test 1.md","category":"I","quote":"Lower Costs - The receiving scheme has a lower overall cost structure compared to your existing pension, meaning more of your savings remain invested for growth rather than being lost to charges.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P21","source":"Suitability Report Test 1.md","category":"I","quote":null,"cells":["Zurich - P10026-719-001/DL","Switch to Aviva Platform","0.50%","0.44%","-0.06%"]},{"passageId":"P10","source":"SIPP research report.md","category":"G","quote":null,"cells":["CT Universal MAP Balanced Fund C Acc","-","£159.77  (0.29%)","£145.05  (0.26%)","-","£304.82  (0.55%)"]},{"passageId":"P10","source":"SIPP research report.md","category":"G","quote":null,"cells":["Total aggregated fund costs:","£0.00  (0.00%)","£258.93  (0.24%)","£147.80  (0.13%)","£0.00  (0.00%)","£406.74  (0.37%)"]},{"passageId":"P22","source":"Suitability Report Test 1.md","category":"I","quote":null,"cells":["HSB