e excess. It states that benefits paid more than two years after death may also attract income tax. It also notes that death before 75 benefits are normally paid tax-free, with caveats around the lump sum and death benefit allowance. The suitability report itself (I) references the forthcoming IHT changes in 2027 that will bring pensions into estates, and notes the client's current IHT liability is £0. The pension switch research report (G) identifies that the new solution day-one death benefit is £106,880.60 versus the existing £110,185.60, a shortfall of £3,305.00 immediately after the switch (the initial advice charge). The Flexi-access drawdown section in the suitability report confirms the 25% tax-free lump sum (subject to the £268,275 Lump Sum Allowance) and that each subsequent withdrawal is taxed at marginal rate of Income Tax, but this appears in a generic product description section rather than being personalised to the client's circumstances. No passage in the file addresses the MPAA being triggered on crystallisation into FAD.","discrepancies":["The report states death benefits before age 75 are paid 'free of tax' without qualification, but the Key Features document clarifies that if the lump sum and death benefit allowance is exceeded, the beneficiary may still pay income tax on the excess — the report's death-benefit tax statement is materially incomplete relative to the product literature.","The report's FAD description (page 9) notes the client 'understands pension freedoms and prefers FAD' but does not state that crystallising into FAD triggers the MPAA (currently £10,000), which is a material tax consequence for a client who is still employed as an HGV driver and making pension contributions — this consequence is absent from both the report and retrieved passages.","The report states death benefits after age 75 are taxed at 'the beneficiary's marginal rate of tax' but does not mention the 45% charge applicable where payment is made to a trust or personal representatives, which is stated in the Key Features document.","The report's small-pot tax explanation describes a '£200 pension fund withdrawal' whereas the actual encashment value is £211.32 — a minor but technically inaccurate personalisation.","The suitability report body (page 5 area) references that pensions will form part of estates for IHT from 2027, which is a material prospective tax consequence of retaining pension assets, but no explanation is given of what this means for the client's position or what mitigating steps, if any, were considered.","The Key Features document explains HMRC's lump sum allowance (£268,275) and lump sum and death benefit allowance (£1,073,100) and their effect on tax-free benefit limits, but the report does not personalise these allowances to the client's situation or confirm whether any lifetime allowance protection applies, even though the client is approaching crystallisation.","The report states tax relief at 20% basic rate (£80 net becomes £100 gross) without confirming the client's tax band; the client earns approximately £300/week net plus state pension of £230/week, making their marginal rate relevant to this illustration."],"comparisonPerformed":true,"missingInputs":[],"analysis":"The report addresses several tax topics: a generic caveat, death benefits taxation (pre- and post-75), tax relief on contributions, and the small-pot encashment taxation with a personalised net receipt estimate. These are positive features. However, several material tax consequences identified in the product literature and the file are absent or inadequately treated. Most significantly: (1) The death benefit tax-free statement on page 9 omits the lump sum and death benefit allowance caveat that appears in the Key Features document — this is a material omission relative to the product literature. (2) The MPAA trigger on crystallisation into FAD is entirely absent from the report. For a client who is still employed and potentially contributing, this is a material tax/regulatory consequence of the recommended action. No passage in the file addresses it. (3) The forthcoming IHT changes to pension assets (2027) are mentioned in the report body but no consequence or client-specific analysis is provided. (4) The small-pot figure is described as '£200' when the actual value is £211.32 — a trivial difference but technically inaccurate. The guard notes that a tax consequence stated without the client's tax band established is unverifiable rather than necessarily wrong; the basic-rate tax relief illustration is therefore not itself a finding. The death-benefit omission and MPAA absence are the most material issues. The MPAA is particularly significant given the client's employment status. Severity is assessed as Moderate: risks are stated but generically in places, and a product-specific material consequence (MPAA trigger) is entirely absent, while the death-benefit tax statement is incomplete relative to the Key Features document.","citations":[{"passageId":"P3","source":"Suitability Report Test 1.md","category":"I","quote":"of the IHT return and may use new options for settling any tax due directly from pension funds. Currently, your Inheritance Tax liability will be £0. We will continue to monitor your IHT position at our future reviews.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P6","source":"Suitability Report Test 1.md","category":"I","quote":"Flexi-access drawdown provides a flexible and tax-efficient means of drawing benefits from a pension fund. An individual can take up to 25% of the pension fund being crystallised as a tax-free lump sum at outset (subject to the Lump Sum Allowance of £268,275 for people with no form of lifetime allowance protection). The remainder of the fund remains invested within a tax advantaged environment from which the individual can take regular or ad-hoc income withdrawals to suit their needs and circumstances. There is no upper limit to the amount the individual can withdraw as an in