st/benefit comparison table is evidenced in the pack for Scottish Widows (£211.32), Standard Life (£3,002), or Aviva existing pension (£3,744.36). These are smaller plans but the absence of any comparison for them is a gap.","5. The report states employer contributions will be lost on People's Pension transfer and recommends retaining it. The People's Pension file (P1–P8, P10) confirms employer contributions are active. However, the report does not quantify the employer contribution rate or amount being preserved, nor confirm the scheme is still active at advice date — only historical transactions to mid-2023 are evidenced.","6. The canonical model records employerContributionsLost as false for the Zurich plan (arrangement 4 being the main transfer). The Zurich policy file (P11) confirms employer payments to the plan total £0.00, consistent with this. No discrepancy on this point.","7. An internal inconsistency in the extraction notes the total of individual plan values (£116,998.47) does not reconcile with the transfer value used in the switch comparison (£110,185). The pension switch report (P22) uses £110,185.60 as the existing pension value, suggesting only the Zurich plan is included, not all five plans combined. The report does not explain this."],"comparisonPerformed":true,"missingInputs":["Confirmation of whether the Zurich plan holds capital units (needed to resolve whether the conditional exit charge applies).","Quantification of ongoing employer contribution rate/amount into People's Pension to confirm what benefit is being preserved by retaining it.","Plan-specific cost/benefit comparison documentation for Scottish Widows, Standard Life and Aviva existing pension."],"analysis":"The report correctly identifies and addresses the most material replacement issue: active employer contributions into the People's Pension, which the file corroborates (P1–P8, P10 show a continuous stream of employer contributions). The recommendation to retain that plan is appropriate and evidenced. For the primary transfer (Zurich, ~£103,439), the pension switch report (P22) provides a full comparison including projected values at three growth rates, reduction in yield, critical yield and effect of switching — this is adequate plan-specific analysis for the largest plan. The Zurich policy file (P13) confirms no exit penalty on transfer ('There are no deductions taken from the current value if benefits are transferred or taken'), which corroborates the report's nil-penalty statement. However, P11 simultaneously discloses a conditional capital-units exit charge and a 5% bid-to-offer spread. The bid-to-offer spread applies to payments, not transfers per se, but combined with the capital-units ambiguity, the report's unqualified 'no charges for transfer' statement for Zurich is not fully corroborated. The guard 'A nil exit penalty confirmed by the provider and left out of the report is not a finding' applies only where the provider confirms nil — here the provider document contains both a confirmation of no deduction and a disclosure of a conditional charge, so the guard does not fully discharge the concern. For the three smaller plans (Scottish Widows £211, Standard Life £3,002, Aviva £3,744), no plan-specific cost/benefit analysis is evidenced in the pack. These are individually small, but collectively £6,957.68, and the absence of any comparison is a gap. The featuresOrGuaranteesLost path being NotPresentInDocument means the report does not address what (if any) valuable features are lost on the four transferred plans. No vulnerability factors are identified in the evidence as materially affecting this assessment. Overall: the main Zurich analysis is present; the employer contribution issue is handled correctly; but the conditional Zurich exit charge/capital units ambiguity is unresolved, the bid-to-offer spread is not disclosed as a switching cost, and the three smaller plans have no plan-specific comparison. These are moderate concerns rather than highest-severity, as the Zurich plan has a comparison, employer contributions are addressed, and the smaller plans are of low individual value.","citations":[{"passageId":"P18","source":"Suitability Report Test 1.md","category":"I","quote":"If you were to transfer away, you would miss out on the valuable benefit of your employer's contributions.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P11","source":"Zurich Policy Info.md","category":"E","quote":"If this plan has capital units, there will be an exit charge applied if the fund is transferred. This exit charge is a standard term of the contract. The charge is taken in order to recover the charges, which would be due, if the fund were to remain invested with Zurich until the customer has reached their selected retirement age (SRA). The charge is taken by reducing the number of capital units paid out; the charge reduces by around 0.3%-0.4% a month until SRA where there is no charge. The charge is capped at 1% from age 55.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P13","source":"Zurich Policy Info.md","category":"E","quote":"There are no deductions taken from the current value if benefits are transferred or taken. The initial set up charges have already been paid.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P12","source":"Zurich Policy Info.md","category":"E","quote":"Bid to offer spread 5% of all payments","cells":null},{"passageId":"P22","source":"Pension switch report - updated.md","category":"G","quote":null,"cells":["Existing pension:","£110,185.60"]},{"passageId":"P11","source":"Zurich Policy Info.md","category":"E","quote":"Currently, no payments are being made to this plan.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P11","source":"Zurich Policy Info.md","category":"E","quote":null,"cells":["Employer payments :","£0.00","£0.00"]},{"passageId":"P1","source":"Peoples Pension Policy Info.md","category":"E","quote":null,"cells":["Employer Contribution (March 2022)","£12.39","£0.00","£12.39","784.1300","1.5801"]},{"passageId":"P20","source":"Test Case 1 - Policy