{"groupId":"G9.1","requirement":"Comparison of existing versus recommended product and investments — each dimension the report compares must be supported by provider or research documentation.","reportSays":"The suitability report compares five existing pension arrangements against the recommended Aviva Platform Personal Pension across multiple dimensions: (1) People's Pension vs recommended — Charges: 0.50% fund + £4.50 annual admin vs 0.44% (0.21% platform + 0.22% IM), Better; FundRange: 7 funds vs 80+, Better; RiskAlignment: Risk level 9 (up to 85% shares) vs Risk level 5 Balanced, Better; AdviserServicing: not allowed vs allowed, Better; RetirementOptions: FAD/Full and partial UFPLS vs FAD/Full and partial UFPLS, Equivalent. (2) Aviva (SP50163762) vs recommended — Charges: 0.50% fund charge vs 0.44%, Better (-0.06%, -£2.25); FundRange: 80+ (lifestyling fund) vs 80+ Aviva platform, Better; RiskAlignment: Risk level 2 lifestyling fund (moves outside agreed risk range) vs Risk level 5 Balanced, Better; AdviserServicing: not allowed vs allowed, Better; RetirementOptions: FAD/Full and partial UFPLS vs FAD/Full and partial UFPLS/FAD, Equivalent. (3) Zurich vs recommended — Charges: 0.18% fund + £2.12/month admin (0.93% total) vs 0.44%, Better (-0.49%, -£506.85); FundRange: 60+ funds vs 80+, Better; RiskAlignment: Risk level 7 Managed AP, no risk-5 funds, blend of risk 7 and risk 2 required vs Risk level 5 Balanced, Better. Additional dimensions for Scottish Widows and Standard Life are also stated. The report asserts no safeguarded benefits exist for any of the five plans. A Pension Switching Report (Defaqto) is referenced for cost/benefit and maturity value projections. The report notes the initial adviser charge is £3,305 taken from the new solution and an ongoing 1% adviser charge. The report states the switch results in a required outperformance of 4.24% (£4,672) relative to the Zurich plan alone, while separately claiming the overall switch is justified by lower charges, better risk alignment, wider fund range and adviser servicing capability.","fileSays":"The case file contains: (1) Policy Research document [P1][P8][P13] covering all five existing plans with fund range, adviser charging, exit penalties, retirement options and other features. (2) Suitability Report pages [P16][P23] reproducing the existing arrangements comparison table showing fund values, charges, fund counts, adviser servicing, retirement options, safeguarded benefits and other considerations. (3) Pension Switch Report [P3][P4][P5][P9][P10][P11][P12][P15][P17][P20] containing maturity value projections at low/mid/high growth rates for each plan switched, the effect of switching, critical yields, reduction in yield, alternatives comparison (including Aviva-only, Standard Life-only, Zurich-only scenarios), stakeholder pension comparison and a ranking of top 10 solutions. (4) Aviva Policy Info [P18] confirming fund value £3,744.36, no guaranteed rates, no GMP, Lifestage Investment Approach (auto-switching), plan status paid-up. (5) Performance charts [P6][P14] comparing Aviva existing vs recommended vs Zurich vs benchmark. The switch report shows the new solution ranks 5th by projected maturity value (£125,000 at mid growth) behind the existing solution (£127,000) and even the Zurich-only retained option (£128,000). At all growth rates the new solution produces lower projected values than retaining all existing pensions. The effect of switching on the overall portfolio at mid growth is -£2,000 or -1.6% decrease. Alternatives (Aviva-only, SL-only, Zurich-only) are all documented. Stakeholder pension is compared. Benefits lost on switch: 'No benefits were listed for the existing products' per [P17]. Adviser charge impact: initial £3,305 taken from new solution (day-one shortfall in death benefit of £3,305) per [P12][P17]. Ongoing 1% adviser charge included in projections. Policy research [P8] shows exit penalties: People's Pension — No; Scottish Widows — Yes; Aviva — No (called provider); Standard Life — No; Zurich — No. Scottish Widows has an exit penalty noted but the report appears to recommend encashing/retaining rather than switching Scottish Widows. The switch report ranks new solution below existing solution at every growth rate. The report acknowledges a required outperformance of 4.24% but the switch report projections show the new solution underperforming at all growth scenarios. The Pension Switching Report fund values used (existing pension £110,185.60 per [P12]) differ from the sum of all five plans (£116,998.47) and from the four plans being switched (£116,998.47 minus People's Pension £6,601.55 = £110,396.92), creating an unexplained £211.32 discrepancy (likely Scottish Widows excluded) but a residual £0.32 rounding difference. People's Pension is a live workplace scheme with employer contributions (£897.13 PA) per [P16]; the report recommends retaining it. Standard Life shows 300 funds available per [P23] but the report states this as a positive for the recommended arrangement (80+ funds) without noting Standard Life's 300-fund range is superior.","discrepancies":["1. The report states People's Pension has 7 funds available [comparison dimension FundRange]. The policy research [P23] shows '7' for People's Pension fund count, consistent with the report. No discrepancy here — removing this after noting it is consistent.","2. The report states Standard Life has retirement option 'FAD' only [P23]; the report's comparison for Standard Life lists existing as 'FAD only, no UFPLS' and recommended as 'FAD/Full and partial UFPLS', assessed as Better. The policy research [P1] shows Standard Life FAD: Yes, UFPLS: Yes — this appears to contradict the report's claim that Standard Life does not offer UFPLS. The suitability report table [P23] shows Standard Life retirement option as 'FAD' only, but [P1] shows 'Full and partial UFPLS available: Yes' for Standard Life. This is a discrepancy between the report and the policy research evidence.","3. The report asserts for Scottish Widows: retirement options 'Full and partial UFPLS' (existing); policy research [P1] shows Scottish Widows FAD: No, UFPLS: Yes — broadly consistent. No material discrepancy.","4. The report claims for Zurich: 'Does not allow adviser servicing'; policy research [P8] shows 'Can adviser charging be applied: No' for Zurich — consistent.","5. The report claims for Standard Life: 'Allows Servicing Adviser: Yes'; policy research [P8] shows 'Can adviser charging be applied: Yes' for Standard Life (column 3, identified as Aviva existing) — the column alignment in [P8] is ambiguous, but [P23] explicitly shows Standard Life 'Allows Servicing Adviser: Yes', consistent with the report.","6. The Pension Switching Report [P9] ranks the new solution 5th (£125,000 mid-growth) behind the existing solution (£127,000) and multiple alternatives. The report asserts the switch is justified on charges, risk alignment and adviser servicing, but does not clearly disclose that the projected maturity value of the new solution is lower than retaining all plans, at every growth rate.","7. The report states the Zurich comparison shows a charge improvement of -0.49% (-£506.85). The switch report [P17] shows at mid growth the effect of switching the overall portfolio is -£2,000 or -1.6% decrease even after the charge saving. The report does not resolve this apparent contradiction — lower charges yet lower projected maturity value.","8. The report asserts the required outperformance to justify the Zurich switch is 4.24% (£4,672). The switch report [P17][P9] shows the new solution requires a critical yield of 3.2% at mid growth just to match the existing solution (all plans combined), not 4.24%. These figures appear to relate to different bases (Zurich-only vs all plans), creating potential confusion about the true hurdle rate.","9. The report states for the Aviva existing plan (SP50163762): 'The plan itself is 0.06% more expensive than the recommended solution' (per the internal inconsistency noted in the canonical model extraction). The charges comparison dimension states 0.50% existing vs 0.44% recommended = -0.06% saving (Better). The switch report [P20] for Aviva-only shows both existing and new solution at 0.6% reduction in yield, suggesting comparable charges rather than a 0.06% improvement — the Defaqto switch report does not corroborate a meaningful charge saving on the Aviva plan in isolation.","10. The report does not address the Scottish Widows exit penalty. Policy research [P8] shows 'Any exit penalties: Yes' for Scottish Widows. Although Scottish Widows is recommended for encashment/retention rather than a full switch to Aviva Platform, the exit penalty is not disclosed in the comparison table or replacement analysis.","11. Standard Life is reported as having 300 funds available [P23] — more than the recommended Aviva Platform's 80+ funds. The report does not note this as a dimension on which the existing Standard Life arrangement is superior to the recommended solution; the fund range comparison for Standard Life is presented as Better for the recommended arrangement.","12. The transfer value used in the Pension Switching Report (£110,185.60 per [P12]) does not match the sum of the four plans being switched (Zurich £103,439.24 + Aviva £3,744.36 + Scottish Widows £211.32 + Standard Life £3,002 = £110,396.92), a discrepancy of approximately £211.32 — likely reflecting exclusion of Scottish Widows from the switch, but not clearly reconciled in the report."],"comparisonPerformed":true,"missingInputs":[],"analysis":"The suitability report provides a multi-dimensional comparison of existing versus recommended arrangements for all five plans, supported by the policy research document, the Defaqto Pension Switching Report, Aviva policy documentation and performance data. The comparison framework is substantially evidenced. However, several material concerns arise: (1) Standard Life UFPLS availability — the report's comparison states Standard Life's existing retirement options are 'FAD only' and treats this as a disadvantage relative to the recommended solution. But the policy research [P1] shows UFPLS is available on Standard Life. This is a one-sided presentation of a comparison dimension that the evidence does not support. (2) Standard Life fund range — Standard Life offers 300 funds [P23], which exceeds the recommended Aviva Platform's 80+. The report presents the fund range dimension as Better for the recommended solution for Standard Life without acknowledging that Standard Life's range is superior. This is a one-sided comparison contrary to the methodology requirement. (3) The new solution produces lower projected maturity values than the existing solution at all growth rates [P9][P17]. The switch is justified on non-monetary grounds (risk alignment, charges, adviser servicing), but the switch report ranking and projections are not clearly disclosed or reconciled in the suitability report in a way that addresses the lower projected outcome. (4) The Scottish Widows exit penalty [P8] is not disclosed in the comparison or replacement analysis. (5) The transfer value discrepancy between the switch report (£110,185.60) and the sum of individual plan values is unexplained, though this is likely a presentation/rounding issue with Scottish Widows exclusion. (6) On the Aviva-only comparison, the Defaqto switch report shows both existing and new at 0.6% RiY [P20], not corroborating the 0.06% charge saving the report claims. The false-positive guards note that fund range and option data changes over time, which partially mitigates the Standard Life UFPLS and fund-count discrepancies, but the policy research was conducted contemporaneously with the advice and is the primary evidence relied upon — it directly contradicts the report's presentation of these dimensions. The vulnerability overlay does not appear to create additional concerns specific to the replacement analysis beyond those already identified. The combination of: (a) a one-sided comparison on Standard Life UFPLS availability contradicted by evidence; (b) Standard Life fund range presented as inferior when evidence shows it is superior; (c) the new solution ranking below the existing solution at all growth rates without clear disclosure; and (d) a missing exit penalty disclosure for Scottish Widows, collectively constitute material concerns that the replacement rationale is not fully supported and contains evidence-contradicted claims.","citations":[{"passageId":"P1","source":"Test Case 1 - Policy Research -2025.md","category":"G","quote":null,"cells":["Full and partial UFPLS available","Yes","Yes","1","3"]},{"passageId":"P8","source":"Test Case 1 - Policy Research -2025.md","category":"G","quote":null,"cells":["Any exit penalties","No","Yes","1","11"]},{"passageId":"P9","source":"Pension switch report - updated.md","category":"G","quote":null,"cells":["5","New Solution","0.6%","£125,000"]},{"passageId":"P9","source":"Pension switch report - updated.md","category":"G","quote":null,"cells":["2","Existing Solution","0.3%","£127,000"]},{"passageId":"P17","source":"Pension switch report - updated.md","category":"G","quote":"No benefits were listed for the existing products","cells":null},{"passageId":"P17","source":"Pension switch report - updated.md","category":"G","quote":"Initial adviser charge of £3,305.00 which is taken from the new solution, reducing the initial investment accordingly.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P12","source":"Pension switch report - updated.md","category":"G","quote":null,"cells":["Existing pension:","£110,185.60"]},{"passageId":"P12","source":"Pension switch report - updated.md","category":"G","quote":"This results in a shortfall in the death benefit immediately after the switch of £3,305.00","cells":null},{"passageId":"P16","source":"Suitability Report Test 1.md","category":"I","quote":null,"cells":["Charges","0.50% fund and £4.50 annual admin charge","0.19% fund and 0.10% platform"]},{"passageId":"P20","source":"Pension switch report - updated.md","category":"G","quote":null,"cells":["Reduction in yield","0.6%","0.6%"]},{"passageId":"P23","source":"Suitability Report Test 1.md","category":"I","quote":null,"cells":["Number of funds available 7","","29","80+","60+","300"]},{"passageId":"P23","source":"Suitability Report Test 1.md","category":"I","quote":null,"cells":["Retirement Options","FAD, Full and partial UFPLS","Full and partial UFPLS","FAD, Full and partial UFPLS","Full and partial UFPLS","FAD"]},{"passageId":"P18","source":"Aviva Policy Info.md","category":"E","quote":"This plan doesn't have any guaranteed rates, including annuity rates","cells":null},{"passageId":"P18","source":"Aviva Policy Info.md","category":"E","quote":"Yes, Lifestage Investment Approach","cells":null}],"severity":"Moderate","outcome":"PotentialConcern"}

